"""ops.py — the resolved operator table: mangled C++ symbol → torch op and tier.

Resolving a symbol to the overload it denotes is deterministic, so it is done ONCE, offline,
and the answer is written down here rather than recomputed in every worker. Doing it live
meant a candidate search, a policy filter and a repair heuristic in the hot path, with an
answer that depended on the order candidates happened to arrive in.

Each entry is `symbol: (op_name, tier, note)`. Three tiers:

  core        lowers to a .pte, and the portable runtime registers this op's own kernel
  composable  lowers only because to_edge decomposes it into core ops
  n/a         unusable — the generator will not emit it

For the two usable tiers `note` is provenance: how the op_name was decided (`signature` =
matched against the constraint signature, `table-name` = fell back to the upstream symbol
table). For `n/a` it is the reason: a pipeline stage that failed (`export`, `lower`,
`transformation`, `eager`, `emit`), or `nograph` when no valid call could be built at all.

HOW THIS FILE WAS PRODUCED — the tiers are a snapshot of one ExecuTorch/PyTorch build, not a
specification, so after an upgrade it has to be rebuilt. The build tooling is not kept in the
tree, so the method is recorded here:

  1. For each constraint folder, read its `_PARAMS` — the signature the constraints were
     extracted against.
  2. Find the torch overload whose schema MATCHES that signature. Candidates start from the
     upstream entry_points.tsv (mangled symbol -> op name), but every overload of each
     candidate's packet is searched, because that table names one op per C++ function and is
     sometimes wrong about which overload — it lists `eye_out_cpu(long, long, Tensor&)` as
     `eye.out`, a two-argument op, when the match is `eye.m_out`, which it never names.
     Matching must compare TYPES as well as names: `add.Tensor` and `add.Scalar` are both
     `(self, other, alpha)`. The model has no enum types, so ScalarType / Layout / Device /
     MemoryFormat all arrive as plain int codes and must compare equal to `int`.
  3. VERIFY by generating a single-op graph and exporting it to a .pte, and record the
     outcome as the tier. `core` vs `composable` is decided by whether the portable runtime
     registers that op's own .out kernel; anything that produced no .pte is `n/a`.

Of 1339 symbols, 1214 resolved by signature and 15 fell back to the table's own name.
"""

